<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olympe de Gouges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1793">1793</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Marie Gouze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olympe de Gouges was a French playwright and political activist, best known for her Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen. Writing plays in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from the 1780s, she was also one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s earliest public opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Her declaration of 1791, an answer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a>, advocated equal rights for women. Associated with the Girondins, she opposed the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>; her writings against the Montagnards led to her arrest, and she was guillotined in November 1793.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olympe-de-gouges.jpg" alt="Olympe de Gouges" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Kucharsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marie-Olympe-de-Gouges.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marie Gouze was born on 7 May 1748 in Montauban in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Her father is uncertain: she may have been the daughter of her mother's husband, Pierre Gouze, a butcher, or of the Marquis de Pompignan, a relationship she encouraged rumours of and which is considered plausible but historically unverifiable. On 24 October 1765 she was married, against her will, to Louis Yves Aubry, a caterer. Their son Pierre was born on 29 August 1766, and Aubry died that November in a flood of the river Tarn. She never married again, calling marriage 'the tomb of trust and love', and she took the name Olympe de Gouges.</p><p>In 1768 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> businessman, Jacques Biétrix de Rozières, funded her move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. There she frequented salons and met writers and future politicians such as Brissot and Condorcet. She published a novel in 1784 and began a prolific career as a playwright; a record of the papers seized at her arrest lists about 40 plays.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Her work expressed indignation at social injustice. She wrote on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, divorce, marriage, debtors' prisons, children's rights and work schemes for the unemployed, and with Condorcet she is considered one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s earliest public opponents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. She published under her own name, unlike many women playwrights at the Comédie Française. She published 68 pamphlets; her first political brochure appeared in November 1788, and in early 1789 her Remarques Patriotiques proposed social security, care for the elderly and a jury system.</p><p>In 1791, answering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a>, she wrote the Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen, which follows the same seventeen-article structure. It contains her statement that a woman has the right to mount the scaffold and must equally have the right to mount the speaker's platform. She followed it with a Contrat Social proposing marriage based on equality between the sexes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>De Gouges welcomed the Revolution but grew disenchanted when equal rights were not extended to women. She was associated with the moderate Girondins and opposed the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. After the Montagnards imprisoned prominent Girondins on 2 June 1793, her increasingly vehement writings culminated in the poster Les Trois urnes, which led to her arrest. She was guillotined on 3 November 1793. On 15 November Pierre Gaspard Chaumette cited her as a warning to women who meddled in politics. Largely forgotten afterwards, she was rediscovered through a political biography by Olivier Blanc in the mid-1980s, and a square in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was named after her in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olympe de Gouges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Olympe de Gouges</b> answered the French declaration of 1789 with a Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen in 1791, and in 1792 Mary Wollstonecraft published A Vindication of the Rights of Woman.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In France <b>Olympe de Gouges</b> answered with her Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen in 1791; she was guillotined during the Terror in 1793.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Olympe de Gouges</b>'s Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen of 1791 follows the seventeen articles of the 1789 text point for point, and Camille Naish has described it as &quot;almost a parody&quot; of the original.</blockquote><a href="/work/declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In October 1789 the market women of Paris marched on Versailles, and in 1791 <b>Olympe de Gouges</b> published her Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancien-regime-france">Social Hierarchy of Ancien Régime France</a></li><li><blockquote>The playwright <b>Olympe de Gouges</b>, author of the Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen, was executed during the Terror for writings attacking the Jacobins.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-3">3 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Montauban, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.02,1.36&amp;year=1777" aria-label="Montauban on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4582 605 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Olympe de Gouges"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5032 875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5032 875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.02,1.36&amp;year=1777">What was here in 1777? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
